First part: Containerization using Docker

# Use of Ubuntu through Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL):

Ubuntu was used as the development environment through the Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL), which provides native access to Linux tools within Windows without requiring a virtual machine. This choice ensures better compatibility with Docker and open-source development tools, improves system stability, and aligns the development environment with typical production systems, which are predominantly Linux-based.

After installing Ubuntu on WSL, all development tasks were performed within the Linux terminal. The workflow included cloning the GitHub repository, creating the Dockerfile and entrypoint script, committing and pushing the changes to a dedicated branch, building the Docker image, and running the container with appropriate port mapping to expose the FastAPI and Streamlit services on the host machine.

# Containerization and Version Control of a FastAPI–Streamlit Application on Linux
1. Introduction

The objective of this work was to containerize a Python-based application composed of a FastAPI backend and a Streamlit frontend, and to manage the project using GitHub for version control. Containerization ensures portability, reproducibility, and ease of deployment across different environments, while GitHub enables collaborative development, version tracking, and code sharing.
All operations were performed on a Linux environment using the command line interface.

2. Environment Setup
2.1 Git Installation and Configuration

First, Git was verified and configured to ensure proper version control:

git --version
git config --global user.name "UserName"
git config --global user.email "user@email.com"

