A dedicated feature branch was used to isolate development work:

git switch -c features-task7-Rajae

2.2 Docker Installation and Login

Docker was installed and enabled on the Linux system:

sudo apt install docker.io
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker


Docker installation was verified:

docker --version


The user authenticated to Docker Hub in order to enable image publishing:

docker login

3. Project Structure Verification

The project structure was verified to ensure the presence of the application modules and dependencies:
ls

Files included: README.md  api  config.py  data  models  plots  src

4. Creation of Docker Configuration Files
4.1 Entry Point Script

An executable shell script was created to start both FastAPI and Streamlit services inside the container:

nano entrypoint.sh


Content of the file:

#!/bin/bash
# Launch FastAPI and Streamlit in the same container

# Start FastAPI in the background
uvicorn api.app:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000 &

# Start Streamlit in the foreground
streamlit run src/dashboard.py --server.port 8501 --server.address 0.0.0.0

The script was made executable:

chmod +x entrypoint.sh

4.2 Dockerfile

A Dockerfile was created to define the container image:

nano Dockerfile

Content of the file:
FROM python:3.9-slim

WORKDIR /app
COPY . .

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir pandas scikit-learn fastapi uvicorn joblib streamlit requests

EXPOSE 8000
EXPOSE 8501

# Run the entrypoint script
CMD ["./entrypoint.sh"]

This file specifies the base image, working directory, dependency installation, application copy, exposed ports, and startup command.

4.3 Requirements
The requirements.txt file contains a list of all the Python libraries required to run the project

nano requirements.txt

Content of the file:

uvicorn
fastapi
pydantic
joblib
pandas
requests

5. Version Control and GitHub Integration

The new configuration files were added to the Git repository:

git add Dockerfile entrypoint.sh requirements.txt
git commit -m "Add Docker configuration for FastAPI and Streamlit"
git push origin features-task7-Rajae


Authentication was performed using a GitHub Personal Access Token, as password authentication is no longer supported.

6. Docker Image Build

The Docker image was built locally using the following command:

docker build -t fastapi-streamlit-app .


After successful completion, the image was verified:

docker images

7. Container Execution and Port Mapping  

The container was executed with port mapping to expose the services to the host machine:

docker run -p 8000:8000 -p 8501:8501 fastapi-streamlit-app


The application became accessible through the browser:

FastAPI documentation:
http://localhost:8000/docs

Streamlit interface:
http://localhost:8501

The running containers were monitored using:

docker ps

8. Image Persistence and Reuse

Docker images remain stored locally even after system shutdown. container ID: b14f6fb7eec8. To verify the availability of images:

docker images

The application can be restarted without rebuilding the image:

docker run -p 8000:8000 -p 8501:8501 fastapi-streamlit-app

9. Conclusion

This workflow demonstrates a complete pipeline for managing a data-driven application using GitHub for version control and Docker for containerization. The approach guarantees reproducibility, simplifies deployment, and allows the application to be executed consistently across different environments. The use of Docker images ensures persistence and easy reuse, while GitHub enables collaborative development and traceability of changes.



Second part: Local Deployement
1- Tag the Image for GitHub Registry:
docker tag diabetes-app:latest ghcr.io/pantherpale-svg/fastapi-streamlit-app:v1/diabetes-app:v1

2-Push the Image to GitHub
echo "<GITHUB_TOKEN>" | docker login ghcr.io -u pantherpale-svg --password-stdin
docker push ghcr.io/pantherpale-svg/fastapi-streamlit-app:v1/diabetes-app:v1

The image is publicly available in the Packages section
Image name: fastapi-streamlit-app

3-Download and Run the Application Locally
docker pull ghcr.io/pantherpale-svg/fastapi-streamlit-app:v1
docker run -p 8000:8000 -p 8501:8501 ghcr.io/pantherpale-svg/fastapi-streamlit-app:v1

FastAPI documentation:
http://localhost:8000/docs

Streamlit interface:
http://localhost:8501

I attempted to deploy the application on cloud platforms using free services such as GitHub Pages and Vercel. Since Vercel does not support Streamlit, I separated the backend and frontend components, but the deployment still did not work. However, these platforms were ultimately not suitable because our application is dynamic rather than static.
I also explored other cloud providers, including Render, Fly.io, and several others, but they require a paid subscription to complete the deployment.

